  "slug": "zero-dna-mirrordna-8a09",
  "summary": "Five preprints test whether gene and variant ranking evidence is informative, using channel separation, sharp bounds and baseline comparisons.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is auditing computational evidence used to prioritize genes and variants. Zero-shot virtual knock-out rankings from observational single-cell data reflect network out-degree rather than causal gene effects. An Open Targets re-ranking for Alzheimer and Parkinson disease separates genetic and protein evidence channels and improves curated-gene recall. For variant effect predictors, Manski sharp bounds show that unscored variants leave many ClinVar and benchmark comparisons undecided, including comparisons involving Evo 2. Pretrained decision models are likewise judged against baselines, being competitive on text tasks but behind a trained mass-spectrometry re-scorer. The new element is explicit quantification of when evidence is uninformative, via bounds, baselines and channel separation, rather than headline accuracy. The SHAP-FOL-ILP proteomics study contributes a rule-based interpretability route in lung adenocarcinoma.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
